From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blurbery <271863866+blurbery@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: presentation timing and frame pacing

First part of the AVFoundation video output for iOS and tvOS (the output
itself arrives in 0025). These modules decide when and how frames are
presented; they make no layer calls.

clock.c holds the playback rate estimator and the media-mode timebase
servo. The estimator divides how far media time advanced by how long mpv
says those frames last, with a fast window for large speed changes and a
500 ms averaging window for small ones; a display-synced frame whose ideal
duration is below 1 ns gives it nothing. The servo anchors the timebase on
the first frame, after a reset, recovery or resume, while paused, when the
nominal rate changes and when the phase error exceeds 50 ms; otherwise it
slews the rate with a proportional and integral term limited to 1.5% (the
integral accumulated over nanosecond steps), writing a new rate at most
every 200 ms and only for a change above 100 ppm. Media times convert to
nanoseconds with one fused multiply-add, saturating beyond the int64 range.

timebase.c wraps the CoreMedia timebase on the host time clock: seeding for
host mode (host time at rate 1) and media mode (time 0 at rate 0), reading
in nanoseconds and anchoring against the source clock, with the host clock
read after the source clock for the offset to the target time.

pacing.c holds the readiness wait before each enqueue (each pass checks
readiness, then the give-up moment 4 ms before the target, then whether the
layer needs recovery, and only then polls again after 1 ms), deadline
drops, the not-ready run and the 500 ms throttle on display layer recovery.
The renderer and the clock are reached through callbacks so the rules can
be tested with a fake renderer.

Applies to mpv v0.41.0 with Vivid's earlier patches.

